ef 17592186124868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124509 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Nullity of a Matrix Using Row Reduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124510 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f3a0e3b9-931a-52bd-9ed9-b8001c8de67b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1045 "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124684 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e7c42f54-2ba5-576a-b2e1-2db695e51ec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124876 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124876 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124876 #edb/ref 1071 "If $D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,$ then $\\text{nullity}(D)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124876 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a0b3d6d-593b-5e4e-ba8b-3eae5b6b11d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124876 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that by definition, $\\text{nullity}(D)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D)).$\n\nTo find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D))$, we reduce $D$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D))=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125385 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125385 #edb/ref 1061 19982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125385 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8764d6f6-efe5-555a-9b58-2ea9897c7843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125385 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125386 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125386 #edb/ref 1061 19983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125386 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1781ce33-0951-5cf7-ba14-893c43247e6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125386 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125387 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125387 #edb/ref 1061 19985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125387 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32808740-b993-5939-9a08-6bc3eab6463c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125387 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125388 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125388 #edb/ref 1061 19986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125388 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "966d2e1c-4409-5d1b-996d-fad6a9c4d98c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125388 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125389 #edb/ref 1061 19987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125389 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b0649dd-72fb-5d4a-a416-f1ca94874689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125389 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125778 #edb/ref 1064 "We already learned that the rank of a matrix $A$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ Now, can we obtain any information from the *non-pivot* columns?\n\nThe answer is yes! The non-pivot columns help to find the dimension of the null space of a matrix $A,$ and here's how.\n\nConsider the matrix \n![](images/99/991b6f0c69e6341620139db9bfce5303785be21760eb8d630bfee92c57d4bc6a.svg)\n\nThe matrix $A$ is already in row echelon form, where the $1$st and the $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd one is a *non-pivot* column. Now, to get the null space of $A,$ we need to find the solution of the system\n\n![](images/1c/1cd56dc26ad4f478c3d7cb14375b8b2e45ef68e3215e79494d56108fecec39cb.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and ${\\color{blue}x_3}$ are basic variables and $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable. Hence, we obtain that \n\n![](images/56/56587dc5c1c98493060edb3561c13aa1576187461418619684459dd1f55626fe.svg)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))={\\color{red}1}$.\n\nIn other words,\n\n$\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is equal to the number of *non-pivot* columns in the corresponding row echelon form of $A$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$  is the number of *free variables* in the corresponding homogeneous system with the coefficient matrix $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125778 #edb/ref 1065 3520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125778 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7c75fc81-c464-5eea-bb66-37258ca64cb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125778 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125779 #edb/ref 1064 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix $A$ is often called the **nullity** of $A$. We will also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125779 #edb/ref 1065 3521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125779 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e46fe003-9e24-5794-b139-5c2805d463c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125779 #edb/ref 1067 "The Nullity of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317749 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317749 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317749 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38fc6530-da0e-5ef8-9489-487b09a244dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317854 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317854 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317854 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56588881-20d6-50d9-9ff9-b07f78c8d62a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317966 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317966 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317966 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74eb3b2a-e28b-5f5f-8bad-16800439d2bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318213 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318213 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318213 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c40e8a26-c0ea-5391-8ea7-86af9f58fbcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318281 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318281 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318281 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d79b5d41-92e6-55a5-93af-01f4431ff8c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186317966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318941 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7f6e57d-01d6-53e7-aeb3-fe75d269ce0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319318 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319318 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319318 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319318 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "831c003e-6ab4-5abb-a995-8aece30d7ab6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319318 #edb/ref 1074 "To find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))$, we reduce $A$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319521 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319521 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319521 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28e68488-3044-5580-9e3c-089a9a1d218e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319553 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319553 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3481e207-e3b3-5e18-a809-b68d786d6e91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319559 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319559 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319559 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "35be0dfc-5948-5bd3-9dac-1c20570872cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319609 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319609 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319609 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49662fce-308a-5973-9daf-90f661164bb4" #edb/ref 175921860454